package helios:system@0.1.0;

/// The machine's human-input devices, handed one at a time to one
/// instance each.
///
/// Helios drives every input device itself — an event ring nobody drains
/// is a ring the host runs out of buffers on, and the next keystroke has
/// nowhere to go — so the kernel owns each device for as long as the
/// machine runs. What this interface hands out is the right to *read*
/// one: exactly one instance holds a device at a time, and the device
/// goes back to the kernel's own drain when that instance lets go or
/// dies.
///
/// The vocabulary is evdev's, unchanged. An event is a `(kind, code,
/// value)` triple, a device describes itself by the codes it reports for
/// each kind, and a burst of triples that belong to one physical moment
/// — a pointer that moved in both axes, a key pressed while a modifier
/// was held — is closed by a `SYN_REPORT`. Nothing here translates that
/// into a model of its own: every consumer would have to undo the
/// translation, and there is no fact about input a Helios-specific
/// encoding could carry that evdev does not.
///
/// # What a slow reader loses
///
/// A device's events are relayed through a queue as deep as the driver's
/// own event ring, so a reader that stops reading cannot pin the ring
/// and stall every other device on the machine. What it loses when the
/// queue is full is a *whole report*: the kernel commits a report or
/// drops it, never half of one, so a pointer never keeps one axis of a
/// move it lost the other half of. Reports dropped that way are counted
/// and published on `helios:system/stats`.
interface input {
  /// Why an input request was refused.
  enum error {
    /// This machine has no input device, or the kernel never brought
    /// one up.
    unavailable,
    /// No device of that name on this machine.
    no-such-device,
    /// Every device of that name is already held by somebody.
    already-claimed,
    /// A handle that outlived a release names a device its instance no
    /// longer holds.
    not-claimed,
    /// The device faulted rather than refused.
    device-fault,
  }

  /// One input event: a Linux `struct input_event` without its
  /// timestamp, which is what a device puts on the wire.
  ///
  /// `kind` is an `EV_*` type, `code` names a code within that type's
  /// namespace, and `value` is what the code reports — a key's state, a
  /// relative displacement, an absolute position.
  record input-event {
    kind: u16,
    code: u16,
    value: s32,
  }

  /// The codes one device reports for one event type, as the bit per
  /// code the device publishes.
  ///
  /// The length is the device's own: it publishes as many bytes as its
  /// highest code needs, and a code past the end is one this device does
  /// not have. Byte `n` bit `b` is code `n * 8 + b`.
  record event-type {
    /// The `EV_*` type these codes belong to.
    kind: u16,
    /// The device's bitmap for that type. Empty for a type the device
    /// declares without codes, which is a fact about the device and not
    /// an absence: a keyboard declares `EV_REP` exactly that way to say
    /// that it repeats held keys itself.
    codes: list<u8>,
  }

  /// One absolute axis a device reports, and the range it reports over,
  /// as `struct input_absinfo` describes it.
  ///
  /// `fuzz` is the noise the device's own filtering leaves behind,
  /// `flat` the dead zone around the resting position, and `res` the
  /// units per millimetre (or per radian, for a rotational axis) — zero
  /// where the device does not know its own scale.
  record abs-axis {
    code: u16,
    min: s32,
    max: s32,
    fuzz: s32,
    flat: s32,
    res: s32,
  }

  /// Everything a device says about itself, read once while the kernel
  /// brought it up and immutable afterwards.
  record capabilities {
    /// The name the device presents itself by, which is the name
    /// `claim` asks for.
    name: string,
    /// Every event type the device declares, in the order it declared
    /// them, with the codes it reports for each.
    ev-bits: list<event-type>,
    /// Every absolute axis the device reports, in ascending code order.
    /// Empty on a device with no absolute axis, which is every keyboard
    /// and every relative pointer.
    abs-info: list<abs-axis>,
  }

  /// One instance's exclusive hold on one input device.
  ///
  /// Dropping it — or dying while holding it — hands the device back to
  /// the kernel's own drain, and the next caller of `claim` may have it.
  resource device {
    /// What this device says about itself.
    capabilities: func() -> capabilities;

    /// Every event this device reports, from now on.
    ///
    /// `SYN_REPORT` is in the stream like any other event: the frame
    /// boundary belongs to whoever is interpreting the events, and a
    /// consumer that acts on a pointer's first axis before the report
    /// closes draws a diagonal as a staircase.
    ///
    /// One stream per device. A second call hands back a second reader
    /// of the same queue, and an event goes to exactly one of them, so
    /// a consumer that wants its events in one place asks once.
    events: func() -> stream<input-event>;

    /// Turn one of the device's indicators on or off.
    ///
    /// `code` is an `LED_*` code the device reports for `EV_LED`; this
    /// is the one direction in which events travel towards the device.
    set-led: async func(code: u16, on: bool) -> result<_, error>;
  }

  /// Every input device the kernel brought up, whether or not somebody
  /// holds it. Empty on a machine that has none.
  available: func() -> list<capabilities>;

  /// Take exclusive ownership of the device `name` names.
  ///
  /// The second caller is refused rather than queued: a compositor
  /// waiting for a keyboard another compositor holds is a provisioning
  /// mistake, not a shortage. This is the rule `display.claim` and
  /// `device.claim` both follow.
  ///
  /// A machine with two devices of the same name — two identical
  /// keyboards — hands out the first free one, so claiming that name
  /// twice yields both of them.
  claim: func(name: string) -> result<device, error>;
}
